questions.answer:
A flaky test passes and fails intermittently, usually from timing, ordering, or environment issues. Reduce it with auto-waiting, deterministic selectors, isolated test data, and no fixed sleeps.

code:
await expect(page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Save' })).toBeVisible();
await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Save' }).click();

real example:
Replacing page.waitForTimeout(2000) with Playwright actionability waits removes the most common fixed-delay flakiness in UI suites.

Data attributes like data-testid are stable contracts between tests and developers, while class names change for styling and break selectors silently.

code:
page.getByTestId('checkout-button').click();
// vs a brittle ,class list like  .btn-primary.rounded

real example:
Teams agree that test hooks use data-testid so refactors of CSS never degrade the E2E suite.

Register listeners before the trigger so the dialog event is captured, then dismiss or accept it, otherwise the browser throws unhandled-dialog errors.

code:
page.on('dialog', dialog => dialog.accept());
await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Delete' }).click();

real example:
Confirming a native alert before clicking the destructive button prevents the mid-click dialog from hanging the action.

Promise.all rejects at the first failure and returns values on full success, while allSettled always resolves with per-promise status so you can inspect every outcome.

code:
const results = await Promise.allSettled(
  endpoints.map(url => fetchStatus(url))
);

real example:
Setup that seeds accounts wants to fail fast with all, while a health-check report wants every endpoint's status via allSettled.

The page object model centralizes selectors and actions for a page in one class, so tests read like business steps and selector changes update in one place.

code:
class LoginPage {
  constructor(page) { this.page = page; }
  async login(user, pass) {
    await this.page.getByLabel('Username').fill(user);
    await this.page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Sign in' }).click();
  }
}

real example:
A renaming of the sign-in button updates LoginPage once instead of across every spec that logs in.

Send the request with the request fixture or fetch, assert the status and body shape, then drive the UI and compare the rendered result against that contract.

code:
const res = await request.get('/api/config');
expect(res.status()).toBe(200);
expect(Number(res.body.featureFlags.length)).toBeGreaterThan(0);

real example:
If the API returns total count in the body, the UI total must equal that number, so a single source of truth reduces conflicting expectations.

JSON.parse throws a SyntaxError for malformed input, so wrapping it lets you report a clean message instead of a raw parser crash that hides the real contract violation.

code:
try {
  const data = JSON.parse(responseBody);
} catch {
  throw new Error('Server returned invalid JSON');
}

real example:
A server-side error page returned as HTML triggers JSON.parse to throw, and the try/catch turns it into an explicit assertion failure.

Test frameworks provide hooks: beforeEach and beforeAll in Jest and Vitest, or Playwright fixtures that inject fresh state per test.

code:
beforeEach(() => {
  localStorage.clear();
});

real example:
Playwright uses fixtures like const test = base.extend({ user: async ({}, use) => {...} }) so every test gets isolated data.

A unit test isolates one function or module with mocks and runs in milliseconds, while an E2E test runs the full browser stack and verifies real user flows end to end.

code:
// unit: expect(calculateTax(100)).toBe(10);
// e2e: await page.goto('/checkout'); await expect(page).toHaveText('$110');

real example:
Teams cover pricing with unit tests on the calculator and a thin E2E layer that confirms the string actually renders on the checkout page.

A retry policy reruns a failed test a bounded number of times to filter transient infrastructure flakes, configured in the test runner rather than inside the test logic.

code:
defineConfig({
  retries: process.env.CI ? 2 : 0,
});

real example:
CI flakiness from slow first-load requests drops when Playwright retries failed specs, while local development stays fast with zero retries.

console.error prints a stack to stderr, and uncaught console errors in a browser session are often asserted as page errors in E2E frameworks.

code:
page.on('pageerror', (err) => {
  console.error('page threw:', err.message);
});

real example:
Listening to pageerror catches silent script failures that break flows, even when the visible assertion alone passes.

page.evaluate runs a function in the browser context and serializes its return value back to Node, enabling checks on browser-only state like window.cookies or local geometry.

code:
const width = await page.evaluate(() => {
  return window.innerWidth;
});
console.log(width);

real example:
Asserting a responsive breakpoint applies measures layout with evaluate, then compares the returned width against the expected design value.
